WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.351
Welcome to Lecture 0 of the

2
00:00:02.427 --> 00:00:05.313
FrontierCS 2 course, the introduction.

3
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:06.300
I'm Boxi Yu.

4
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:09.510
FrontierCS 2 asks whether AI agents can

5
00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:11.930
do research in computer science.

6
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.010
Most coding benchmarks ask

7
00:00:14.076 --> 00:00:15.540
an agent to fix a bug

8
00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:17.602
or to pass a test that already

9
00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:18.600
has an answer.

10
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:21.237
FrontierCS 2 asks for research.

11
00:00:21.311 --> 00:00:23.598
Each task takes a paper and the

12
00:00:23.672 --> 00:00:25.590
code its authors released.

13
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.382
It removes the paper's key contribution

14
00:00:28.382 --> 00:00:30.580
and asks an agent to write it back.

15
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.606
To pass, the agent's version must beat the

16
00:00:33.669 --> 00:00:35.771
authors' own code on every metric

17
00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.810
of the task's hidden workloads.

18
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.612
The tasks come from thirteen fields

19
00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:42.711
from machine-learning systems and

20
00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:45.366
high-performance computing to cryptography

21
00:00:45.428 --> 00:00:46.230
and robotics.

22
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.657
Machine learning and databases

23
00:00:48.726 --> 00:00:50.180
have nine tasks each.

24
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.969
Theory, bioinformatics and software

25
00:00:53.038 --> 00:00:54.850
engineering have one each.

26
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.280
That makes forty-eight tasks.

27
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.741
Sixteen models each run every task once

28
00:01:00.741 --> 00:01:03.230
seven hundred sixty-eight runs.

29
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.834
A private set of more than one hundred

30
00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:07.496
further tasks is kept back

31
00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:08.860
to be announced later.

32
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.842
Each model runs in its own agent harness

33
00:01:12.842 --> 00:01:15.498
Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code

34
00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:18.070
the GPT-6 models in Codex CLI

35
00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.140
and Gemini 4 Argon in two harnesses

36
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.800
Antigravity and Pi.

37
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.195
So a leaderboard row is a model

38
00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:26.910
and its harness together.

39
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.860
Pause and predict.

40
00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.441
Gemini 4 Argon appears twice

41
00:01:31.518 --> 00:01:33.749
with pass rates of fifty-four

42
00:01:33.825 --> 00:01:35.210
and forty percent.

43
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.560
How can the same model score so differently?

44
00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.390
The harness is part of what is measured.

45
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.222
One example: under Pi

46
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.782
the provider's content filter refused

47
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.938
a request and Pi ended the run

48
00:01:47.938 --> 00:01:50.700
in ten of its forty-eight runs under Pi.

49
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.930
Antigravity retries refused requests.

50
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.130
A task starts from a paper's own repository.

51
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.051
The builders delete the

52
00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.554
paper's contribution

53
00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.193
the queue logic in BBQ

54
00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:04.764
the search in MetaOpt

55
00:02:04.764 --> 00:02:07.360
the scheduling decision in Shockwave.

56
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.460
The rest of the code stays

57
00:02:09.525 --> 00:02:11.275
so the agent works inside a

58
00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:12.830
real research codebase.

59
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.121
The authors' code is the human reference

60
00:02:16.121 --> 00:02:17.340
the bar to beat.

61
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.040
A run has a wall-clock budget of five hours.

62
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.404
The agent sees its repository

63
00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:25.070
and the task statement.

64
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.986
For each patch it submits as a train call

65
00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.696
it gets back a score on the

66
00:02:29.758 --> 00:02:32.140
development workloads, and one message.

67
00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.340
The evaluator

68
00:02:33.401 --> 00:02:35.709
the hidden workloads and the reference

69
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:36.984
stay with the judge

70
00:02:37.045 --> 00:02:39.474
and the hidden evaluation adds workloads

71
00:02:39.535 --> 00:02:40.810
the agent never sees.

72
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:41.820
At the end

73
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.980
the agent picks one of its versions

74
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.480
as its final submission.

75
00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.492
Only that version is scored

76
00:02:47.553 --> 00:02:49.030
on the hidden workloads.

77
00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.170
Choosing that version is part of the work.

78
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.349
Here is a toy run of our own

79
00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:55.800
with five submissions.

80
00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.693
The agent saw only the development scores

81
00:02:58.693 --> 00:03:01.236
and the best one is forty-four point two

82
00:03:01.236 --> 00:03:02.870
at minute two hundred ten.

83
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.900
Pause and predict.

84
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.530
Should the agent submit that version?

85
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.951
Now the hidden scores

86
00:03:09.951 --> 00:03:11.902
which the judge measures later

87
00:03:11.902 --> 00:03:13.730
against a reference of forty.

88
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.355
The best development version was tuned

89
00:03:16.415 --> 00:03:18.150
to the development workloads

90
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.707
and on the hidden ones it

91
00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.400
scores only thirty-six: below the reference.

92
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:23.776
The last version

93
00:03:23.776 --> 00:03:25.948
at minute two hundred seventy-five

94
00:03:25.948 --> 00:03:27.698
scored lower in development

95
00:03:27.698 --> 00:03:29.086
forty-two point eight

96
00:03:29.146 --> 00:03:30.715
but forty-one point six on

97
00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:33.310
the hidden workloads: above the reference.

98
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:34.401
In this toy

99
00:03:34.463 --> 00:03:36.563
the hidden score stands in for the

100
00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:38.540
full check that decides a pass.

101
00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.746
The benchmark reports what the agent chose

102
00:03:41.746 --> 00:03:42.410
as it is.

103
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.298
An agent may end on a version that fails

104
00:03:45.298 --> 00:03:47.960
although an earlier one would have passed.

105
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.140
So, three things.

106
00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.141
A task is a paper with its

107
00:03:52.204 --> 00:03:53.791
key contribution removed

108
00:03:53.791 --> 00:03:56.330
and the authors' code is the reference.

109
00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.444
During its run, the agent can see only the

110
00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:00.760
development scores.

111
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.830
And only the version it picks is judged.

112
00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.587
As an exercise: if you could see only the

113
00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:07.761
development scores

114
00:04:07.761 --> 00:04:09.522
which version would you pick

115
00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:11.518
and what would make you trust the

116
00:04:11.577 --> 00:04:13.220
last one more than the best?

117
00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:16.210
Next: what counts as a pass.
